From: GREG NEWTON <gnewton@ozemail.com.au>
Date: Sun, 25 Sep 94 19:07:37 +1000
Subject: [#94-9-473] Functional Motivation For Evolution?

from gnewton@ozemail.com.au(Greg Newton)

Reply to message text:

>  From: Reilly Jones <70544.1227@compuserve.com>

>>Evolution is a "dumb" process of mutations being
>>naturally selected by the environment at the time the mutation occurs.
>>Therefore *any* possible mutation can occur and whether it continues or not
>>depends on the local environmental conditions from that time onwards.>

>It is not necessarily true that "*any* possible mutation can occur."

>Stuart Kauffman in "Origins of Order" writes:  "..a tendency of
>evolution to occur in preferred directions not because of selection constraints
>but because the underlying system has preferred directions of change in the 
face
>of random  mutations."

> The idea that evolution is dumb is just another scientific working hypothesis,
> although many people seem to grant it a strong degree of religious faith.

Here we are again granting a degree of "mysticism" to our universe - If 
evolution
has *preffered* directions of change - how does *it* decide what *it* prefers?

In evolution the mutations are not chosen *before* they occur - but *after*, by 
the 
natural selection process.  To imply that evolution is capable of preffering or 
choosing a mutation before it occurs - is infering that evolution has a mind 
back of
it.  Sorry folks but I'm a complete "A-Theist:- The Absence of Theistic belief" 
 I do 
not subscribe to the notion of a supernatural being controlling this show - 
who's 
every whim can alter the way the universe operates - what chaos that would be!
Furthermore, extreme mutations *do* occur - that are not on the "pathways" that
Kauffman talks about - If the mutations find the environment harsh enough - 
they 
simply do not survive! Hence natural selection.

There is no religious faith in the fact that evolution is a process of mutation 
and
natural selection - quite the contrary!  There is no mind/creator/creature/ or 
"intelligence" involved in the process - it is a process that merely follows 
natural 
laws that have always existed - the apparent "preferences" are simply a result 
of the process, as is the apparent "intelligence".

Greg Newton.
"To explain the unknown by the known is a logical procedure, To explain 
the known by the unknown is a form of theological lunacy"
                                                    - David Brooks, The 
Necessity of Atheism.













