From: GREG NEWTON <gnewton@ozemail.com.au>
Date: Sun, 25 Sep 94 19:07:30 +1000
Subject: [#94-9-474] Functional motivation for evolution?

From: gnewton@ozemail.com.au(Greg Newton)

>Reply to message text:

>  From: pavel@PARK.bu.edu (Paul Cisek)

>>Also I fail to see that 
>>primitive organisms (without consciousness) can "solve problems in order to 
>>survive" - they can only rely on mutations (which cannot be induced 
>>"artificially" by creatures without conciousness), natural selection makes 
the 
>>rules, and determines who/what wins the game.

>Again, we define "solve" differently.  My meaning comes from the usage of
>"solution" as any scheme for dealing with some task at hand.  Such a
>"solution" may be "discovered" by the brute-force "search" that results
>from the evolutionary process.  (I've put in quotes other words that
>one might be tempted to antropomorphize; please don't).  This again
>implies no goal-seeking.  I would guess that your definition of "solve"
>carries implications of some rational process that depends on consciousness.
>If so, then I question the utility of your definition, since it relies
>upon assumptions made about phenomena that we do not understand.  What
>exactly are "creatures without consciousness"?

Let's not take the words out of their context - "solve problems in order to 
survive" 
indicates the precsence of consciousness and goal-directed action - you are 
saying
that these "primitive organisms" are aware of their need to survive, and that 
they are
aware of any threat to their survival - and will find a solution to this 
problem ("solve"
according to the Collins English dictionary means: To find a solution to a 
problem)
To solve a problem you have to be aware of it, and aware of possible solutions.

To refer to Collins again - "Consciousness - To be aware of ones self, 
environment
and mental activity, and this to a certain extent determines ones choices of 
action.
Once again I will maintain that "primitive organisms" DO NOT posess any form of
consciousness - in just the same way that a molecule involved in a chemical 
reaction
is not aware of itself or its' environment - it merely reacts according to laws 
governing
its' existence.  (Consciousness also includes volition - unlike Skinners' 
faulty definitions).

To talk about primitive organisms solving problems in order to survive is total 
nonsense.

Let's stop all this irrational talk about evolution like it is some sort of 
process with a 
consciousness behind it - or even that individual components of it have 
consciousness,
until mankind came on the scene there has been no consciousness invloved in the 
evolutionary process - we can only marvel at what the natural laws of the 
universe 
have allowed to come about given the ingredients that existed on Earth a long 
time ago.  We the living, conscious beings of Earth, can now discover, learn, 
change 
and create according to our abilities, needs and desires.  If we are to solve 
the problem
of death by discovering how to achieve biological immortality - we have much 
work to 
do, let's get on with defining the knowledge and technology requirements, and 
obtaining
what we need - we must be goal-oriented to be successful. 

Greg Newton.
